<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Serbia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Serbia is a country in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, whose medieval kingdom was strongly influenced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. In the nineteenth century it regained independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and became a rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. The assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archduke-franz-ferdinand-of-austria">Franz Ferdinand</a> by a Bosnian Serb nationalist led to war with Serbia in 1914 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/serbia.jpg" alt="Serbia" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Serbia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Franz Conrad von Hötzendorf's offensive planning was badly at odds with the army's real capabilities: in 1914 the invasions of <b>Serbia</b> were repulsed at Cer and the Kolubara, while in Galicia the army was routed around Lemberg (Lviv), losing much of its trained peacetime officer corps within months.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Tens of thousands of graves excavated in Hungary, Slovakia, Austria and <b>Serbia</b> document the culture of this period, especially the cast bronze belt fittings decorated with griffins and tendril patterns that distinguish the Late Avar era.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Orthodox Christianity that the Byzantine state patronised and defined became the faith of Russia, <b>Serbia</b>, Bulgaria, Romania, Georgia and the Greek world — a “Byzantine commonwealth” that extended the empire's cultural reach far beyond its political frontiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Simeon conquered <b>Serbia</b> in 924 but was defeated by the Croats in 926.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Basic treaties with Romania, Slovakia and Ukraine renounced territorial claims, though the rights of the roughly five million Hungarians living abroad, mostly in Romania, Slovakia and <b>Serbia</b>, still cause tensions; relations with Serbia grew close, while those with Ukraine deteriorated from 2017 over the Hungarian minority there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Greece was the first new nation state carved out of the Ottoman Empire, and its creation by the intervention and guarantee of the great powers set a pattern followed by <b>Serbia</b>, Romania and Bulgaria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
